@meta
id: 30-01
title: Where does this thing come from
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: goto-definition
par: 30
minutes: 5
vscode: F12, or Ctrl-clicking the name
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
This chapter rebuilds the parts of an IDE you actually use — go to
definition, rename symbol, the Problems panel, the diff view — out of
things vim has had since before any of them existed. Start with the one
you press most.

`windowDays` appears in three methods of this provider. Each one declares
its own, and the three values are different on purpose. Finance have
raised a ticket: the refund window on `cancel` is three days and it was
agreed at thirty. The other two windows are correct.

Find the declaration that belongs to the method you are standing in, and
change the number. The trap is that searching for the name from the top
of the file finds whichever declaration comes first, which is not always
the one you want.

@keys
| `gd` | go to the local declaration of the word under the cursor |
| `gD` | go to the first occurrence in the whole file |
| `*` | next occurrence of the word under the cursor |
| `#` | previous occurrence |

@hints
Get the cursor onto a *use* of `windowDays` inside `cancel` first —
searching for `windowDays *` puts you on one.
--
`gd` reads "goto declaration". From a use, it jumps back to where that
name was first declared inside the current function.
--
`/windowDays \*` then `gd`. You land on the declaration in `cancel`.
`f3` to the number, `a0` to make it thirty.

@solution
    /windowDays \*<Enter>  gd  f3 a 0 <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`gd` is not clever. It finds the start of the current function, then
searches forward for the first occurrence of the word, and it ignores
matches inside comments and strings. That is enough to be right about
local variables and parameters nearly all the time, and it costs two
keystrokes with no index, no plugin and no server.

`gD` does the same from the top of the file, which is what you want for
something declared at module scope.

Where they stop being enough is the moment the definition is in another
file — and that is most of them. The next few lessons go after that,
first with the file system, then with a tags file.

One habit worth building now: when you want to know what something is,
do not scroll. Put the cursor on the name and ask vim.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 3;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    if (order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff) {
      return this.close(order, windowDays);
    }

    return this.refund(order);
  }

  public async expire(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 90;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    if (order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff) {
      return this.close(order, windowDays);
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async archive(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 365;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    return order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff ? this.store(order) : order;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 30;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    if (order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff) {
      return this.close(order, windowDays);
    }

    return this.refund(order);
  }

  public async expire(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 90;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    if (order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff) {
      return this.close(order, windowDays);
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async archive(id: string) {
    const windowDays = 365;
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - windowDays * 86_400_000;

    return order.placedAt.getTime() < cutoff ? this.store(order) : order;
  }
}
